
Declarative Operation
==================================================


What is Declarative Operation?
------------------------------------------
Declarative operation is where you use things like YAML
files to describe the system you want, and then K8S attempts
to make it so.

As opposed to *imperative* operation, in which you use
kubectl commands to talk directly to the K8S API Server to
build up the system you want.

Everybody always uses Declarative operation in production
environments.



Scope of the Controller
------------------------------------------

The Skupper Controller can control CRs in either a
single Namespace, or across multiple Namespaces.

That choice is conrolled by the WATCH_NAMESPACES
env var, which is part of the Controller deployment.

The typical behavior is to have it watch all Namespaces.



Helm Chart
------------------------------------------

The Helm chart installs the platform (the Controller, CRDs, RBAC, etc.).
It does *not* create the actual Skupper network configuration.

Use of the Helm Chart keeps the installation of the platform separate 
from the configuration of individual sites and services

You use the Helm Chart something like this:


helm install skupper oci://quay.io/skupper/helm/skupper \
  --namespace west \
  --create-namespace \
  --set scope=namespace


After the Helm install finishes, you still need to apply your own resources:
  * Site
  * Listener
  * Connector
  * Link
  * AccessGrant or AccessToken





Mega-YAMLs
------------------------------------------

Big YAML files that do an entire Skupper install in one
big gulp. These are an alternative to usingthe Helm Chart.

This way is convenient for quick installs but less flexible 
than Helm for customization.

Installed something like this:
  kubectl apply -f https://skupper.io/v2/install.yaml






The Operator
------------------------------------------
Once the Operator is running, it can create and manage Skupper sites in
the cluster. In practice, it acts as a higher-level way to deploy the same
controller that you would install with Helm or the static YAML manifests.

The Operator installs and manages the Controller using the
Operator Lifecycle Manager (OLM).

It creates and manages Skupper sites in the cluster.

  Why do people use Operators?
  {
    Operators are designed for long-lived, managed services. The platform
    can restart, upgrade, and monitor them consistently.

    Operators often come with well-defined permission models that fit
    enterprise governance.

    If the cluster already runs many Operators (Service Mesh, monitoring,
    databases, etc.), using the Skupper Operator keeps the operational
    model consistent.

    (I.e., people use it because they have been using it.)
  }


   Why Helm is usually better:
   {
     * maximum flexibility and simple values overrides.
     * you are on plain Kubernetes (not OpenShift).
     * a lightweight, scriptable installation.
   }




Helm vs Operator
------------------------------------------

  * Helm / YAML → Most common and flexible for general Kubernetes.

  * Operator → Preferred when you are in OpenShift and you want 
    platform-managed lifecycle and updates.





